1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,656
This is CLASP: Contrastive Labelling And System-1 Prediction.

2
00:00:05,006 --> 00:00:13,931
It's a workbench for building fast text classifiers from a few hundred labelled examples, and for making them better with feedback from the people who use them.

3
00:00:14,281 --> 00:00:20,406
In the next few minutes we'll cover what it is and how it works, and then walk through the whole tool, tab by tab.

4
00:00:21,167 --> 00:00:24,413
First, what do we mean by a System-1 classifier?

5
00:00:24,763 --> 00:00:34,743
It's a model that answers one question about a piece of text: which class does it belong to? And it answers by scoring a fixed set of options, rather than writing an answer.

6
00:00:35,093 --> 00:00:45,780
That makes it fast and cheap. A decision takes milliseconds, so it can sit next to a large language model and screen, route or triage everything that model sees and says.

7
00:00:46,130 --> 00:00:52,501
Think of checking every turn of a chat for compliance problems, or sending each support email to the right team.

8
00:00:53,267 --> 00:00:55,465
Here's how CLASP scores a text.

9
00:00:55,815 --> 00:01:01,500
Every class gets a one-sentence description, and the text is compared against each of those descriptions.

10
00:01:01,850 --> 00:01:08,256
Both go through the same encoder, Qwen3-8B, which stays frozen and is never retrained.

11
00:01:08,606 --> 00:01:14,767
On top of it sit two small heads: a state head for the text, and an action head for the class descriptions.

12
00:01:15,117 --> 00:01:22,343
The model measures how similar the two are, turns those similarities into probabilities, and the most likely class wins.

13
00:01:22,693 --> 00:01:29,094
The design comes from CLM-8B, a contrastive language model from Stanford and NVIDIA.

14
00:01:29,867 --> 00:01:33,708
Training uses a contrastive loss over those class descriptions.

15
00:01:34,058 --> 00:01:42,437
For every labelled row, its own class is the positive and every other class is a negative, so you never have to write negative examples by hand.

16
00:01:42,787 --> 00:01:49,006
Only the two heads train: about nineteen million parameters, which takes roughly a minute on a laptop.

17
00:01:49,356 --> 00:01:59,499
So what do you bring? Rows of text and label, a plain sentence describing each class, and one negative class, meaning none of the above, with its own examples.

18
00:01:59,849 --> 00:02:07,192
That last one matters. The model always picks a class, so the negative class is what lets it say that nothing applies.

19
00:02:07,967 --> 00:02:10,713
The tool is organised as a loop of four tabs.

20
00:02:11,063 --> 00:02:17,005
Tab zero, curate: ask Claude Code to research a topic and write a labelled dataset for you.

21
00:02:17,355 --> 00:02:23,291
Tab one, train: pick your data, define the classes, validate it, and train a model.

22
00:02:23,641 --> 00:02:30,024
Tab two, chat and flag: run conversations through the model, and flag every verdict you disagree with.

23
00:02:30,374 --> 00:02:37,592
And tab three, post-train: fold those flags back in, check the new model against the current one, and promote it.

24
00:02:37,942 --> 00:02:40,969
Every model is versioned, so you can always switch back.

25
00:02:41,733 --> 00:02:48,186
The repo ships with one complete worked example: a regulatory screener for a broker-dealer's AI chat.

26
00:02:48,536 --> 00:03:02,777
It labels each turn with the rule it might trigger, such as FINRA 2210 for misleading communications, Reg BI for personalised recommendations, or FINRA 4530 for customer complaints. Or it flags nothing.

27
00:03:03,127 --> 00:03:09,091
On its held-out test set, the five-class model scores a macro-F1 of 0.98.

28
00:03:09,441 --> 00:03:16,786
On messages written to look nothing like the training data, it scores about 0.8, and that's the number to plan around.

29
00:03:17,136 --> 00:03:22,124
Its main weakness is reading blunt Reg BI recommendations as FINRA 2210.

30
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:24,987
Getting started takes one command.

31
00:03:25,337 --> 00:03:33,510
Clone the repository and run scripts/run_ui.sh. The first run sets everything up, then serves the app on port 8710.

32
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:38,359
If you use Claude Code, just open the clone and ask it to run the UI.

33
00:03:38,709 --> 00:03:49,931
You'll need Python, about twenty gigabytes of disk, and either an Apple Silicon Mac or a GPU with twenty-four gigabytes of memory. The demo and training need no API key.

34
00:03:50,281 --> 00:03:52,215
Now let's look at the tool itself.

35
00:03:54,467 --> 00:03:58,620
This is the workbench. It opens on the chat tab, with a short welcome.

36
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:06,165
Across the top are the four steps of the loop, and the model that's currently screening, so you always know which version you're looking at.

37
00:04:06,433 --> 00:04:13,489
The sidebar holds the conversation controls and the screening model selector. It only appears on the tabs that use it.

38
00:04:13,839 --> 00:04:18,568
Hover over any small i icon, and you'll get an explanation of that control.

39
00:04:20,833 --> 00:04:23,581
Let's start at tab zero, data curation.

40
00:04:23,931 --> 00:04:31,020
You describe the classifier you want in plain language, or start from a template. Here we've chosen review sentiment.

41
00:04:31,370 --> 00:04:37,729
You can set how many rows you want per class, which model Claude Code should use, and a hard budget for the run.

42
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:47,572
When you press start, Claude Code runs on this machine in its own folder. The activity feed shows each search, each page it reads, and each file it writes.

43
00:04:47,922 --> 00:04:54,177
It can only search the web and read and write files in that folder: no shell commands, and nothing outside it.

44
00:04:54,433 --> 00:05:03,790
When it finishes, CLASP checks what was written: every label has a definition, there's exactly one negative class, and there are enough rows per class.

45
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:12,867
You can refine the dataset with a follow-up prompt, such as asking for harder examples, or send it straight to the train tab with the classes filled in.

46
00:05:13,217 --> 00:05:22,455
For this recording, Claude Code ran in offline demo mode, so this is a small toy dataset. For the rest of the tour, we'll use the shipped example.

47
00:05:24,733 --> 00:05:33,935
On the train tab, pick a dataset from the project. Your own files in the data folder appear at the top, followed by anything you've curated, and the examples.

48
00:05:34,285 --> 00:05:39,297
We've loaded the regulatory sample: twenty-one hundred rows across five classes.

49
00:05:39,647 --> 00:05:50,012
Each label gets a display name, the description the model scores against, the speaker who can trigger it, and one is marked as the negative class. Here, they come prefilled.

50
00:05:50,267 --> 00:06:00,594
Validation checks for unknown labels, tiny classes, duplicates and conflicting labels, then splits the data into training, validation and test sets.

51
00:06:00,867 --> 00:06:08,923
Training runs in the background. Because the encoder is frozen, each text is encoded once and cached, and only the heads learn.

52
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:18,810
A few seconds later, we have results on the held-out test set: accuracy, precision, recall and F1, next to two baselines.

53
00:06:19,160 --> 00:06:27,987
The linear probe is a sanity check: if it beats the trained heads, something's wrong. The zero-shot row shows the untrained heads, close to chance.

54
00:06:28,267 --> 00:06:35,547
The confusion matrix shows exactly where the mistakes are. Rows are the true class, columns are what the model predicted.

55
00:06:35,897 --> 00:06:39,957
One click activates the model, and it becomes the one screening the chat.

56
00:06:42,233 --> 00:06:49,672
On chat and flag, you can replay scripted conversations, so no API key is needed, or chat with a live model.

57
00:06:50,022 --> 00:06:56,834
Each conversation in the list shows how many turns its author expects to be flagged. We'll play the busiest one.

58
00:06:57,100 --> 00:07:05,247
Every turn gets a verdict. Here the user is complaining about a transfer they didn't make, and the model flags FINRA 4530.

59
00:07:05,597 --> 00:07:10,109
Underneath, the transcript's expected label is compared with the model's answer.

60
00:07:10,367 --> 00:07:14,306
Play all runs the rest of the conversation, and not everything is right.

61
00:07:14,656 --> 00:07:24,827
Here the assistant promises the customer they'll get every penny back. That's a promissory statement, so the author expected FINRA 2210, but the model let it through.

62
00:07:25,100 --> 00:07:29,996
Details shows the probability of every class, so you can see how the call was made.

63
00:07:30,267 --> 00:07:38,680
If you disagree with a verdict, flag it. Pick the correct label, add a note if you like, and it's saved as expert data for post-training.

64
00:07:40,933 --> 00:07:44,717
Tab three, post-train, starts from the selected model.

65
00:07:45,067 --> 00:07:54,756
It lists the flags raised in the chat that fit this model, and you can add a file of corrections from subject-matter experts. We've added the example corrections file.

66
00:07:55,033 --> 00:08:08,312
By default, it continues training the current model gently: a few epochs at a low learning rate, with the expert rows repeated so a handful of corrections can move it, and the original data mixed in so it doesn't forget.

67
00:08:08,567 --> 00:08:18,174
Then comes the gate. The candidate and the current model are scored on the same held-out data, and, for the example, on both out-of-distribution sets.

68
00:08:18,524 --> 00:08:27,507
Any drop in F1, or in recall for a single class, counts as a regression, and promoting past one needs a written reason, which is logged.

69
00:08:27,857 --> 00:08:35,883
Here the candidate improved on both out-of-distribution sets but slipped on one class, so the gate holds it back for a person to decide.

70
00:08:36,167 --> 00:08:46,775
Every model lands in the registry with its version, its parent, its metrics, and its gate result. Our candidate became version two, and any version can be activated again.

71
00:08:49,033 --> 00:08:59,780
Finally, the classes tab draws the selected model's label space: which classes a user turn can get, which an assistant turn can get, and the negative class beneath them.

72
00:09:00,130 --> 00:09:04,644
It's built from the model itself, so it's always right for the version you've selected.

73
00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:11,407
That's CLASP: curate, train, use, flag, and improve, all in one loop.

74
00:09:11,757 --> 00:09:21,877
The README covers setup and the repository layout, the docs folder has the architecture and a developer guide, and every evaluation run lives in the Tests folder.

75
00:09:22,227 --> 00:09:26,679
Drop your own files into the data folder, and build your own classifier.

